  "query_preview": "..A large upper low will remain over the NW United\nStates through the weekend with high pressure to our east. This\nresults in continued south-southwesterly flow over our region.\nSome shallow moisture has been pulled northward into Gila and\nsouthern Navajo/Apache counties, resulting in a few showers. This\nmoisture is forecast to shift eastward through the day, with the\nhighest chances for additional showers and thunderstorms (30-60%)\neast of a Payson to four corners line, with only low chances\n(5-15%) farther west. A few storms could contain heavy rainfall\naround the White Mountains. By Saturday, moisture is forecast to\nincrease from south to north through the day with isolated to\nscattered showers and thunderstorms. Some of this moisture is\nsurging northward from distant Hurricane Marie, which is forecast\nto be around 700 miles west of the southern tip of the Baja \nPeninsula by Saturday afternoon and heading northwestward. \nExtensive cloud cover may limit instability for many areas, so \nstorm intensity remains uncertain. By Sunday, a shortwave \ndisturbance is shown by much of the guidance moving across\nsouthern NV into UT while even deeper moisture is shown in the\nlatest grand ensemble moving into northwest Arizona. Mean precipitable\nwater values from the 00Z ensembles are in the 1.00\"-1.25\" range\nfor much of our area, highest in northwest Arizona. These values\nare in the 85-95th percentile, with values around the 98th\npercentile in northwest Coconino County. Even with extensive cloud\ncover, the shortwave may provide enough lift for some heavier\nshowers and thunderstorms in the northwest corner of the state.\nStorm chances are up to 50-70% in this area which includes the\nGrand Canyon/Supai. WPC has included this area in a slight risk\nfor excessive rainfall/flash flooding on Sunday, and this will \nneed to be monitored closely. The placement of the heavier\nrainfall risk is still not certain, and could end up being farther\nnorthwest into NV/UT. By Monday and Tuesday, rainfall chances\nshift to mainly eastern Arizona. Mid to late next week are looking\nactive again as a favorable monsoon pattern sets up.",
